rsx: tiles bound over main memory are tiled for every RSX access (render targets, textures, NV3089, NV309E, NV308A,
NV0039, vertex, index and fragment program fetches).

Resistance 3 binds tile 4 over main memory (IO 0x30000, 0x580000 bytes, pitch 8192, bank 2). While aiming it
downsamples the scene colour and depth with NV3089 into it (two 480x352 images at 0x30000 and 0x30800), an SPU
depth-of-field job reads and writes them in the tiled layout the RSX gives them, and the RSX samples the job's output
as a texture. Copied in and read back linearly, the job's output landed on rows 0-15 of every 64 (a 1920-byte-wide
rectangle, tiled, covers exactly those linear rows) and showed as horizontal bands over the scene; the blur never
appeared at all.

A tile is an address translation in the RSX's memory interface, so every RSX client sees the region through it
(envytools' NV40 VRAM notes); RPCS3 tiles NV3089 destinations and texture reads only. This does it for all of them
through one layer:

- rsx_tiling.h: the tiled address of a byte, a port of RPCS3's rsx::tiled_dma_copy (link and commit in the file).
- rsx_tiling.c (new): the one table of bound main-memory tiles (rsx_tiling_set; a lock for writers and readers, a
  bit mask so a client pays one load while none is bound), range copies (rsx_tiled_read / rsx_tiled_write: a range
  may start before a tile, cross several and end past them; guest memory reached an IO page at a time, so IO pages
  scattered in host memory are right; a `store` hook for clients that must notify SPU reservations), and the
  footprint of a range (whole 64-row bands rounded to the 64 KB bank groups), for change detectors.
- cellGcmSetTile binds the tile, as RPCS3's does (tile.bound = pitch > 0): the game never calls BindTile. Set, Bind,
  Unbind and cellGcmInit publish to rsx_tiling_set; local tiles are transparent (RPCS3 skips them too).
- cellGcmSys: NV3089 (either side), NV309E's swizzled copy (its source), NV0039 (either side) and NV308A inline
  words go through the range copies when they touch a tile; writes keep gcm_store's reservation notify.
- rsx_draw_engine: texture uploads, guest_wrote and the surface seed read through the tiling; guest_read and the
  zeta store write through it; vertex, index and fragment program fetches read detiled copies from a per-draw arena
  (the fetch plan keeps pointers for the whole draw); texture change hashes cover the footprint.
- Not covered: rsx_live_draw.c (its guest reader comes from its caller), reports and semaphores in a tile.

test_rsx_tiling (tools/test_rsx_tiling.py; now built with rsx_tiling.c): addresses for four pitch classes against a
separate port of the RPCS3 function, the tiling permutes the tile's 32-byte blocks, a row round-trips, a range
across two tiles, scattered IO pages, an unbacked page (zero-filled, reported), a local tile, unbinding, and every
byte of several ranges inside their footprint. test_rsx_draw_engine: targets, textures, an indexed draw's indices
and vertices and a fragment program in a tiled region go through the tiling. test_nv3089_engine_read: NV3089 into
and out of a tile, NV309E out of one, NV0039 lines each way, an NV308A inline word. Each new check fails with the
tiling turned off. The fixtures that compile cellGcmSys.c or rsx_draw_engine.c in include rsx_tiling.c.
In the game (Haven street fight, aiming): no bands, the depth-of-field blur appears; a dump of the tile at the
texture read, detiled, is clean on every row. Only the NV3089 and texture paths reach the tile there (a logged run);
NV309E, NV0039, NV308A and the fetches are covered by the tests above. Cost: about 1.1 ms of CPU a frame while
aiming (tiled copy and texture read). The tile lookup each fetch now makes takes ~9 ns while a main-memory tile is
bound (1 ns otherwise); the game keeps tile 4 bound and makes ~400k such lookups a second in that fight, about
0.2 ms of the render thread a frame. A/B aiming runs (3 each, against the NV3089-and-engine-only version) gave
medians of 19.9 and 19.4 fps, within the noise of that machine.

Upstream: not proposed yet.
